    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i'm curious about the specific biology of it though... like how does the actual timing of the holds affect the vagus nerve or cortisol? is there some kind of chemical shift happening?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Box breathing, along with other forms of diaphragmatic breathing, stimulates the vagus nerve, which plays a critical role in the parasympathetic nervous system. The pattern of holding your breath for four seconds and releasing for four seconds helps maintain a steady, deep nasal breath, providing frequent signaling to the brain. This signaling helps reduce cortisol levels in the body — a stress hormone — and supports an anti-inflammatory response, theoretically improving your physical and mental health responses to stress. By consciously controlling your breathing, you can influence autonomic functions and chemical balances, promoting relaxation and a sense of well-being.",
      "source": "target",
